---
import DevLayout from '../layouts/DevLayout.astro'
---
<DevLayout
  title="Colophon: asystemofcells.dev"
  description="How asystemofcells.dev is built: Astro static in a pnpm workspace, sharing one design kit and one roster with the product site, with no runtime scripts from anyone else."
  canonical="https://asystemofcells.dev/colophon/"
>
  <div class="shell" style="padding-top:3.4rem">
    <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; asystemofcells.dev</a>
    <div class="dev-prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Colophon</p>
      <h1>How this is put together.</h1>

      <p>
        Two sites, one repository. <code>apps/asoc-com</code> is the product
        house and <code>apps/asoc-dev</code> is this. Both are Astro 4, both
        build to static files, and both consume the same two workspace
        packages, which is the point: <code>@asoc/roster</code> holds the single
        <code>roster.public.json</code> that every cell listing on either site
        reads from, and <code>@asoc/kit</code> holds the shared design tokens,
        the head component, and the footer. Neither site can quietly disagree
        with the other about what exists, because neither one owns the list.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this one looks different</h2>
      <p>
        The kit is token-driven rather than class-driven, so a surface changes
        its whole voice by rebinding tokens rather than by forking components.
        The footer at the bottom of this page is the same component the product
        site renders, unmodified. It looks like this because the tokens
        underneath it changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one rebinding that carries the rest is <code>--font-mono</code>. On
        the product site that token is a naming convention rather than a
        typeface classification: it points at Hyle Grotesk, the proportional
        house face, so the marketing site speaks in one voice throughout. A
        terminal surface cannot borrow that. Right-aligned timestamps, aligned
        columns and tabular figures all need real fixed advance. So this site
        binds every type slot to JetBrains Mono, self-hosted, and ends its
        fallback chain in <code>monospace</code> rather than in Hyle. A font
        failure here has to degrade to some mono, never to a proportional face
        that would silently break every column on the page.
      </p>
      <p>
        No Hyle faces load here at all, which also sidesteps a real trap: the
        kit's <code>hyle-fonts.css</code> declares absolute paths under
        <code>/fonts/hyle/</code>, served out of the product site's public
        directory. Importing it on this host would request four faces that all
        404.
      </p>

      <h2>What runs in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        Two small scripts, both first-party, both operating on markup that is
        already in the page: the filter on the home page, and the tissue filter
        on the roster. Every row they act on is server-rendered, so the full
        list is there for a crawler, for <code>curl</code>, and for anyone with
        JavaScript off. Nothing fetches. Nothing is lazy-loaded. There are no
        third-party scripts, no analytics, no cookies, and no serverless
        functions behind this site at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Type and colour</h2>
      <p>
        JetBrains Mono Variable throughout, self-hosted from the build rather
        than requested from a font CDN, because a font CDN request is a
        third-party request. The base size is 13px rather than 16px: mono runs
        optically wide, and most of this site lives between 10 and 13.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ground, ink, hairline and provenance hues are the house values from
        the kit, unchanged. Six section hues are defined on top of them at
        roughly equal apparent lightness against this background. Product accent
        colours are only ever swatches and hairlines here, never text: nine of
        the fourteen fall below 4.5:1 on this ground, and the house rule says
        colour is never the only channel carrying meaning anyway.
      </p>

      <h2>Not here on purpose</h2>
      <p>
        There is no API reference, because there is no public API. The only two
        server functions in the repository are the proxies behind the Nooz
        reader, and their own source comments describe them as an open-proxy
        surface by construction. Documenting them as a product would be an
        invitation to the exact thing that code is already worried about.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also no changelog, no SDK section, and no contributor guide.
        Each of those would be an empty room, and an empty room reads as an
        abandoned building rather than as a promise.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DevLayout>
